## Text structure

### Guideline

- Keep one topic per file: prose on top, appendices and notes at the bottom, so a document can be read front-to-back or diffed cleanly.
- Split by responsibility (e.g. `docs/release-notes.txt`, `docs/api-reference.txt`), not by date or author.
- Keep shared boilerplate (headers, glossaries, style rules) in one place and reference it; never copy-paste it into every document.
<!-- Project-specific / Text-Guideline -->

### Layout

```
├── assets/         # repository assets (images, logos, etc)
├── config/         # global project configuration
├── docs/           # design and user documents (plain text)
├── examples/       # sample documents and snippets
├── scripts/        # helper scripts (text checks, wrapping, generation)
├── test/           # fixtures, sample text, and expected check outputs
├── web/            # rendered or published copies, if the text is hosted
├── Dockerfile      # container build definition
├── Makefile        # build/check task automation (optional)
<!-- Project-specific / Text-Layout -->
```

### Layer Dependencies

Notes for declaring architecture layers in the `Layer Dependencies` section of `.attunic/context.md`; adjust them to the project before declaring.

- Plain-text projects have no importable architecture layers; leave the architecture layer table empty, or use it to document document-level organization instead (e.g. `docs/` depends on `assets/` and the shared glossary; `test/` depends on `docs/` fixtures; nothing depends on `scripts/`).
- Check by hand: a document duplicating content that another owns is a violation; cross-reference instead.
